  "narrative": "Synthesis after validation: The dataset now contains two central validated synthetic-lethality-like associations and one well-supported opposite/weak dependency association. First, H14 is validated: MSI-high lung lines have substantially more negative WRN dependency scores than MSI-low/MSI-indeterminate lines. Discovery R14 and validation R26 give nearly identical signed effects around +0.79, with the validation interval well clear of zero and far above a 10% benchmark for WRN dependency. This is an association in the supplied lung screen dataset, not proof of causal synthetic lethality or external generalizability. Second, H19 is validated in a mechanistically specific refinement: among adenocarcinoma lines without ARID1A loss, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency. Discovery R19 and validation R40 are both large and precise, supporting the robust SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2-dependency finding. H13, the reversed KEAP1/NFE2L2 relationship, also remains supported: KEAP1 loss is associated with less negative, i.e. weaker, NFE2L2 dependency. The H8 response deadline is resolved by rejecting and closing H8 because its direction expected stronger NFE2L2 dependency with KEAP1 loss, whereas discovery and independent validation both support the opposite relationship. Third, H27 is rejected and closed. Its broad EGFR-amplification/EGFR-dependency discovery was only borderline and crossed zero; independent validation R39 is signed in the opposite direction, with an upper bound below the approximate 10% clinical benchmark for EGFR dependency. Thus the evidence does not support EGFR amplification as a clinically meaningful predictor of stronger EGFR dependency in this dataset. The most informative next analysis is the proposed KRAS-wild-type restricted EGFR-amplification test: it can distinguish whether the broad null result was influenced by driver co-occurrence composition or whether EGFR amplification simply lacks a strong direct dependency signal. Supported findings include MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency across multiple robustness subsets, and KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Strong newly accepted dataset association: CDKN2A loss among TP53-wildtype lines is associated with substantially stronger MDM2 dependency, H50; this was the opposite of the originally registered direction and remains active for possible validation. Negative or unsupported findings include BRAF V600E, EGFR amplification broadly, and ERBB2 amplification as predictors of stronger matching dependency in the main and driver-exclusive tests, as well as MYC-amplification effects on POLR2A in high-MYC-expression lines. Unresolved priorities remain broad KRAS-mutation associations, EGFR amplification in driver-exclusive subsets, MYC-related USP7/DHX9/RPL11/STAT3 dependencies, marker/QC controls, and heterogeneity/interaction tests.",
